;; SOUNDNESS REGRESSION FIXTURE.
;;
;; `tick` returns a different value on every call, so `(- (tick) (tick))` is
;; never 0 -- it is -1 -- and the declared `(>= r 0)` is false.  With the gate
;; off the runtime check catches that and the program aborts.
;;
;; It used to NOT abort with `--enable=refined`. Encoding a call as an
;; uninterpreted function makes its occurrences congruent, so both `(tick)`s
;; became the same term `t`, the solver proved `t - t >= 0`, and the runtime
;; check was elided. Turning the experiment on changed a correct program into
;; one that silently returns a value violating its own refinement -- the one
;; thing the design forbids.
;;
;; Congruence is now opt-in: only a callee KNOWN pure gets it -- one whose body
;; passes a default-deny whitelist walk, or a name that resolves to no function
;; at all and is therefore an abstract measure. `tick` is neither (inline C is
;; not on the whitelist, and `#fx{Unsafe}` is a further veto), so each
;; occurrence is a distinct opaque value and the obligation is refuted rather
;; than proved. See the sibling fixture refine-impure-fx-empty for why the
;; effect row alone was not enough evidence.
;;
;; This fixture asserts the DIAGNOSTIC. The behavioural half -- that the check
;; survives and fires -- is what the exit code of the gate-off build showed;
;; here the build must not succeed at all.
(defn tick [] #fx{Unsafe} : int
  ```c
  static int64_t n = 0;
  return n++;
  ```)

(defn always-nonneg [] #fx{Unsafe} : #refine{ r : int | (>= r 0) }
  (- (tick) (tick)))

(defn main [] : int
  (println (unsafe (always-nonneg)))
  0)
